# Directed Self‐Assembly of MOF‐Derived Nanoparticles toward Hierarchical Structures for Enhanced Catalytic Activity in CO Oxidation

## abstract
The controllable self‐assembly of nanomaterials remains a great challenge in nanotechnological applications, especially for the hierarchical structure with high complexity. Herein, by taking the advantage of highly dispersed metal nodes and mild thermal stability of metal‐organic frameworks (MOFs), the self‐assembly of nanoparticles is directed from MOFs to construct CuO hierarchical structures, which have an inherited octahedral framework consisting of the microspheres, nanowires, and polyhedrons, respectively. Unlike the conventional self‐assembly in a solution media (such as solvent and molten solid), the assembly in this work is the first demonstration through a solution‐free approach. Moreover, compared to the general MOF‐derived CuO octahedron, the assembled hierarchical CuO structure exhibits much enhanced catalytic activity in CO oxidation thanks to the exposure of more active sites during the assembly.

The controllable self-assembly of nanomaterials remains a great challenge in nanotechnological applications, especially for the hierarchical structure with high complexity. Herein, by taking the advantage of highly dispersed metal nodes and mild thermal stability of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), the self-assembly of nanoparticles is directed from MOFs to construct CuO hierarchical structures, which have an inherited octahedral framework consisting of the microspheres, nanowires, and polyhedrons, respectively. Unlike the conventional self-assembly in a solution media (such as solvent and molten solid), the assembly in this work is the first demonstration through a solution-free approach. Moreover, compared to the general MOF-derived CuO octahedron, the assembled hierarchical $\mathsf{c u o}$ structure exhibits much enhanced catalytic activity in CO oxidation thanks to the exposure of more active sites during the assembly.
As one of the most challenging scientific issues, the controllable self-assembly of nanoparticles has gained great interest in the last two decades due to their collective properties and the potential applications in photonics, electronics, magnetism, catalysis, energy storage, etc.[1–6] Many isotropic and anisotropic nanostructures including nanospheres, nanowires, nanosheets, and 3D architectures have been successfully fabricated by various nanotechnologies of assembly (e.g., chemical vapor deposition, lithography, DNA-templating, etc.).[2–7] Generally speaking, prior to triggering the self-assembly, nanoparticles should be well dispersed or even monodispersed in the solution to avoid the random aggregation and flocculation during the assembly.[4–7] This prerequisite usually requires the colloid or elaborately chemi­cal modification of the nanoparticle, which not only make the procedure complex and tedious but also increase the difficulty in constructing the hierarchical structure.[2,4,5] Thus, it remains a great challenge to develop a facile and alternative approach to design and manipulate the self-assembly of nanoparticles, especially for the hierarchical structure with multidimension and high complexity.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as a typical self-assembled nanomaterial, have been paid tremendous attention in the last two decades due to their distinct physical and chemical properties.[8] Recently, they have been regarded as prominent sacrificial templates to construct the specific nanostructures of the porous metallic compound. Numerous nanomaterials with specified architecture inherited from pristine MOFs have been achieved by conventionally annealing treatment of MOFs, such as ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ hollow microboxes, $\operatorname{Co}\textcircled{a}\operatorname{N}.\operatorname{C}$ hierarchical rods, and $\mathtt{C u O}$ hollow octahedra.[9,10] However, apart from the stability of the derived structure, the performance is hardly satisfactory since the collapse of the porous structure and the random aggregation may block most of the active sites during the heat treatment.[11] As a result, it becomes more and more controversial to achieve these delicate architectures at the expense of the highly ordered structure. More recently, rather than the special architectures, more attention has been laid on the highly ordered and atomically dispersed metal nodes in MOFs. Sizecontrollable nanoparticles with high dispersion and even single atoms catalyst have been easily achieved by direct thermolysis of MOFs.[12,13] Considering that high dispersion of nano­particle has been demonstrated as a prerequisite to conducting the ordered self-assembly, in our opinion, MOFs would have the gift in designing and building the self-assembled architectures due to their inherent atomically dispersed metal nodes and mild thermal stability.
Herein, we demonstrate that the CuO octahedral architecture consisting of the different assembled structures have been constructed successfully by the dispersed nanoparticles divided from the metal-organic framework (Cu-BTC). The self-assembly behavior was performed in the mixed gas of $2\ \mathrm{vol}\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{He}$ at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and can be easily manipulated by the surface properties of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC. Notably, unlike the conventional self-assembly in a solution media (such as solvent and molten solid), the assembly in our work is the first demonstration through a solution-free approach, which is more straightforward and manageable. Moreover, compared to the general MOF-derived $\mathtt{C u O}$ octahedron, the assembled hierarchical $\mathtt{C u O}$ structures have exhibited much enhanced catalytic activity in CO oxidation thanks to the exposure of more active sites during the assembly.
The geometrical characteristic and crystallographic structure of the product were examined by field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM) and powder X-ray diffraction (XRD). Cu-BTC was prepared following the well-known recipe.[14] The as-prepared particles display an octahedral morphology shown in Figure S1A (Supporting Information). All of the diffraction peaks in Figure  S1E (Supporting Information) can be assigned to Cu-BTC, proving the high purity of the resultant. According to the previous work, the metal compounds tend to aggregate randomly by direct pyrolysis.[10,12] To conduct the ordered assembly, the framework of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC was first loosened in a vacuum oven at $170~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the pretreatment, the blue particle turned to be dark green, accompanied by the appearance of cracks and holes on the surface of the framework shown in Figure S1D (Supporting Information). XRD patterns (Figure  S1F, Supporting Information) displayed the integrity of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC structure and no apparent peak of copper oxides emerged, implying the only loss of short-range order and partially broken of $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ bonds in Cu-BTC during the pretreatment. Subsequently, the product was calcined in a flowing mixed gas of 2  vol. $\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{He}$ to trigger the self-assembly (Scheme 1).
To monitor the evolution of the products, the structural and phase transformations with different thermal treatment durations were captured and shown in Figures 1 and 2. The products obtained after 1, 2, 6, and $24\mathrm{~h~}$ are denoted by Sphere-1h, Sphere-2h, Sphere-6h, and Sphere-24h, respectively. Obviously, microsphere started to form on the surface of the framework at the preliminary stage (Figure  1A). The inserted high-magnification FESEM image in Figure 1A and Figure S2B (Supporting Information) show clearly that, the surface of Sphere-1h was divided into highly dispersed nanoparticles with an average diameter of $18.4\mathrm{nm}$ after $^{\textrm{1h}}$ reaction. XRD pattern declared the
Sphere-1h was mainly made of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (JCPDS card no. 5–0667, Figure  2A). Since the copper in pristine Cu-BTC is divalent, the presence of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in Sphere-1h indicates the occurrence of reduction reaction in low oxygen partial pressure. Considering that the metal-oxygen bonds in MOFs can be partially broken, it can be inferred that decomposition reaction governed the firsthour reaction, in which the $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ coordination bonds may be partially broken under the heating to generate the valence variation from $+2$ to $+1$ .[15,16] More intermediates were collected and exhibited in Figure  S3 (Supporting Information) to reveal the formation mechanism of the microsphere. The exposed nanowires beneath the surface illustrate that, unlike the metal node on the surface, the internal network can’t react with sufficient oxygen, as a result, it further decomposed into numerous nanowires under the heat treatment (Figure S3D, Supporting Information). The interface of the hemisphere and bulk was found to be connected by the nanowire rooted from the framework, hinting the hemispheres grew up from the assembly of divided nanowires inside the framework (Figure S3A, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the infant sphere shown in Figure S3B,D (Supporting Information) illustrated that, as the oxidation proceeded, the nanowires coalesced and produced the nanoparticles which ultimately assembled into the microspheres. To unravel the internal detail of the microsphere in Sphere-1h, the hemisphere was peeled off by grinding and ultrasound treatment, as shown in Figure 3. The FESEM image demonstrated the spherical structure was porous and woven by the dense attachment of nanowires. Correspondingly, the resulting concavities on the exterior also verified the origin of the assembled hemisphere (Figure  S3C, Supporting Information). In accordance with the results of FESEM and XRD, the porosity of the hemisphere can be easily identified from the transmission electron micrograph (TEM) image (Figure 3C). The $d$ -spacing of the adjacent lattice fringe displayed in high-resolution (HR)-TEM image is $0.30\ \mathrm{nm}$ , matching well to the (110) facet of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (Figure  3E). After $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ oxidation, the chemical composition of products including Sphere-2h, Sphere-6h, and Sphere-24h was tested to be CuO (JCPDS card no. 45–0937) supported by the XRD pattern in Figure 2A. However, with prolonged time, microspheres increased gradually until covered the whole surface of the octahedron, with increasing of the average diameter from 1.50 to $2.31~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure  S4, Supporting Information). Distinguished from the initially woven structure of Sphere-1h, the microspheres of Sphere-24h were constructed by nanoparticles with an average size of ${\approx}77.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  3B and Figure  S5, Supporting Information), implying the growth and recrystallization of the nanoparticle. Consequently, the bigger gap between the particles can be easily found in the TEM image (Figure 3D). The lattice distance in Figure 3F was measured to be $0.25~\mathrm{nm}$ , coinciding well with the CuO (002) plane. According to the self-assembly of nanoparticles in the solution, this phenomenon can be attributed to Ostwald ripening, which involves the growth of larger particles at the expense of smaller size.[4,17] Although there was no phase change after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ , it was understandable that the amorphous and poorly crystallized CuO nanoparticles would be prone to recrystallize with the time increase, leading to the enlargement of the particle size in the microsphere.
The Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectrum further confirmed the structural changes and revealed more details of component changes in the products. The band at $623~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is assigned to Cu (I)-O vibration, while the bands of 480, 538, $588~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are attributed to Cu (II)-O vibration.[18] The peaks located at 759, 731, and $1450~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are indexed to $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-C}}$ ring out-of-plane bending modes and the carboxylate symmetric stretch of the organic linker in Cu-BTC, respectively.[19] Figure  2Ba indicates the presence of the residual ligand and incomplete collapse of MOF’s structure in Sphere-1h. In light of the vanishing of the Cu-BTC’s characteristic peaks in the XRD pattern of Sphere-1h, the MOF derived compound should be amorphous except the crystallographic phase of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . In accordance with XRD results, after $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ oxidation, copper in the $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC was completely oxidized, implying the further assembly of the nanoparticles can be mainly attributed to the thermodynamic force. The local coordination information around Cu was furtherly exhibited in X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) on Cu K-edge (Figure 2C,D). The white line at 8997 eV, the shoulder peak at $8985\ \mathrm{eV}_{\colon}$ , and the pre-edge absorption at $8977\mathrm{eV}$ in X-ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) spectra are all assigned to $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ , which are originated from the $1{\mathrm{s}}\to4{\mathrm{p}}$ transition (continuum), $\scriptstyle1\mathrm{s}\to4\mathrm{p}_{\mathrm{z}}$ transition (shakedown) and the $1\mathrm{s}\xrightarrow{}3\mathrm{d}$ transition, respectively.[20] When compared to the $\mathtt{C u O}$ reference, all the products except Sphere-1h are exhi­ biting the similar shape and position of the absorption peaks, validating that the oxidation state of Cu species is the same as the CuO. Due to the full occupation of $\mathrm{d}^{10}$ orbitals, no preedge adsorption is produced in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , however, two intense absorption peaks of $\mathrm{Cu^{+}}$ can be observed at 8980 and $8984~\mathrm{eV}$ as shown in $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reference (Figure 2C). The overall feature of Sphere-1h in the XANES spectrum approximates to $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ reference, suggesting that the average valence of Cu in Sphere-1h is close to $+1$ . Meanwhile, the distinct blue shift implies Cu species in Sphere-1h is more electrophilic which may be caused by the incomplete collapse of the cluster in $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -BTC.[21] The bond information can be further described in the extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectra. As shown in the Fourier transform (FT) of the EXAFS (Figure 2D), the dominant peak around $1.5\mathrm{~\AA~}$ is attributed to the first coordination shell of $\mathrm{Cu-O}$ , while the other main peak between 2.5 and $3.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ is ascribed to the second coordination shell of $\operatorname{Cu-O-Cu}$ in the oxide. The unique $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}$ dimeric units in CuBTC contribute to the characteristic peak located at ${\approx}2.1$ Å.[21,22] In accordance with the XANES results, the presence of the $\mathsf{C u\mathrm{-}C u}$ bond in Sphere-1h indicates the incomplete decomposition of the unit. Correspondingly, the absence of the $\mathrm{{Cu-Cu}}$ bond in Sphere-2h demonstrates the complete collapse of the CuBTC, declaring the reaction-driven assembly during the first $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ oxidation. After that, the consistent coordination environment among Sphere-2h, Sphere-6h, and Sphere- $\cdot24\mathrm{h}$ in EXAFS signifies no more chemical reaction occurred around Cu during the assembly. It demonstrates the following assembly was driven by thermodynamic forces, presenting the typical feature of self-assembly.[1,5]
To evaluate the importance of limited oxygen and the pretreatment, first, the pretreated Cu-BTC was calcined in air at $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ as a comparison. As reported before, the product with a sintered and shrunken morphology was obtained (Figure S7A, Supporting Information),[23] only a few aggregated nanoparticle could be observed on the surface of the framework. It is comprehensible that excess oxygen can accelerate the reaction with metal in Cu-BTC, incurring the rapid collapse of the structure and random agglomeration inside. Thus, no applied force can drive the internal nanoparticle to conduct the assembly on the surface of the crystal. Subsequently, the as-prepared Cu-BTC was directly calcined in $2\ \mathrm{vol}\%$ $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{He}$ without the pretreatment. As shown in Figure S7B (Supporting
Information), apart from a few assembled microsphere on the surface, the whole framework was divided into separated nanoparticles with a size of about $100\ \mathrm{nm}$ . In view of that low reaction rate is indispensable to fabricate the symmetrical microspheres, the limited oxygen, in this case, is definitely beneficial to the spherical assembly.[24] However, without the pretreatment, internal nanoparticles could hardly move freely to assemble into large-sized microspheres, instead, they aggregated with adjacent nanoparticles. According to these results, the formation mechanism can be described as follows: At the early stage, with the help of the pretreatment, metal nodes and the whole network of Cu-BTC was divided into free nanoparticles by the reaction with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . As the time increased, external copper was preferentially oxidated in the limited oxygen, at the same time, internal copper species transferred to the exterior for the oxidation, swelling nanoparticles to the outside. So as to lower the total energy of the system, the raised nanoparticles self-assembled into microspheres. Subsequently, based on the Ostwald ripening, amorphous and poor-crystallized nanoparticles recrystallized in the microspheres, generating the finally porous hierarchical structure.
Since the self-assembly of the dispersed nanoparticles can be regulated by the binding free energy related to the modification of the surface,[25] furtherly, to verify the versatility and tunability of the presented approach, we directed the self-assembly of the derived nanoparticles by the introduction of the additives. As shown in Figure  1E,F, after Au and $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticles were loaded, another two hierarchical structures of CuO octahedra constructed by the assembled nanowires and polyhedrons were successfully achieved. The as-prepared products were denoted by Nanowire-1h and Polyhedron-2h, respectively. XRD patterns demonstrate that Nanowire-1h consists of CuO, $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , and Au, while the Polyhedron-2h is a mixture of $\mathtt{C u O}$ and Pt (Figure S6, Supporting Information). Although the role of the additives during the self-assembly has not been revealed in this paper, we can still conclude preliminarily that the self-assembly of nanoparticles divided from MOFs can be manipulated by the addition of the additives.
The catalytic activity of the as-prepared CuO hierarchical structures was evaluated in CO oxidation. As a comparison, the generally aggregated CuO octahedron was fabricated by calcining Cu-BTC directly as reported in the literature (Figure S8, Supporting Information).[10,23] The conversion curves in correlation with the temperature were plotted in Figure  4A. It declared Sphere-2h and Sphere-6h exhibited the best activity which can convert CO completely to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at $180~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Following that, Sphere- $24\mathrm{h}$ and Sphere-1h can reach the full conversion at 200 and $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively. According to the analysis above, the relatively poor activity of Sphere-1h can be attributed to the incomplete collapse of copper units. Although $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is usually more active than CuO, the undecomposed Cu units in Sphere-1h can make little contribution to the CO oxidation due to the lack of lattice oxygen, resulting in a relatively smaller amount of active sites in Sphere-1h.[15] It is also comprehensible that there is a trade-off between the exposure of active sites and the assembly. On one hand, the assembly can facilitate the migration of the active site from internal to external. On the other hand, the enlargement of the particles during the assembly also reduces the exposure of the active site. This leads to the decrease of activity in Sphere-24h, coinciding with the lowest surface area in the four products (Figure S9 and Table S1, Supporting Information). Remarkably, all the as-prepared products exhibited better catalytic activity than CuO octahedron. Based on the Mars-van Krevelen mechanism, the catalytic activity in CO oxidation is heavily dependent on the exposed active sites which can generate a strong interaction with CO.[26] CO temperatureprogrammed desorption (CO-TPD) was performed to evaluate the activity and amount of exposed active sites. As shown in CO-TPD profiles (Figure  4B), distinguished from the others, Sphere-2h and Sphere-6h display two desorption peaks which are located at ${\approx}250$ and ${\approx}440~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . It means there are two different types of CO adsorption sites in the samples and exhibits a much stronger interaction with CO. More importantly, the larger quantity of CO adsorption in Sphere-2h, Sphere-6h, and Sphere-24h can be easily identified by the peak area, which is 1.11, 1.32, and $1.05~\mathrm{mL~g^{-1}}$ compared to $0.67\mathrm{\mL\g^{-1}}$ of the $\mathtt{C u O}$ octahedron, indicating the more exposed active sites in the $\mathtt{C u O}$ hierarchical structures. The redox properties of the samples were tested by the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ temperature programmed reduction $(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TPR})$ (Figure 4C). The reduction peak of Sphere1h at the highest temperature is consistent with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , while the wide range from 240 to $400~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ indicates the mixture of copper species.[27] Corresponding to the catalytic activity, Sphere-2h, Sphere-6h, and Sphere-24h exhibited a significantly lower reduction temperature than $\mathtt{C u O}$ octahedra, which can be attributed to the relatively small particle size of $\mathtt{C u O}$ in the assembled structure. Finally, the stability of the structures was evaluated by the measurement of catalytic stability (Figure 4D). Sphere-2h and Sphere-24h were kept at 180 and $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The negligible decline of activity in Sphere-24h suggests the good stability of the assembled structure, while $10\%$ activity was reduced in Sphere-2h after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . In view of that the hierarchical structure of Sphere-2h belongs to the intermediate, the decrease can be assigned to the further aggregation of nanoparticle during the reaction. Besides, as shown in Figure S10 (Supporting Information), the hierarchical architectures were both well retained after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ reaction, verifying the stability of the assembled structure.
In summary, this work presents a novel and facile method to direct the self-assembly of MOF-derived nanoparticles toward multiple hierarchical structures. By employing the unique properties of highly dispersed metal nodes and mild thermal stability of MOFs, the controllable hierarchical architectures, which are octahedrons consisting of the microspheres, nanowires, and polyhedrons respectively, can be easily achieved by the self-assembly of nanoparticle divided from MOFs.
The behavior of assembly can be regulated by the surface pro­ perty of MOFs. Pretreatment and low concentration of oxygen have also been proved to play a crucial role in the construction. Interestingly, unlike the regular self-assembly of nanoparticles in solvent and molten solid, this controllable assembly was realized in a solution-free approach. Compared to the randomly aggregated $\mathtt{C u O}$ octahedra, the orderly assembled hierarchical structures have exhibited much enhanced catalytic activity in CO oxidation, benefiting from the exposure of more active sites during the assembly. In light of the wide application of MOF-derived material in energy storage and conversion, the facile methodology would provide a new perspective to design various hierarchical structures as well as a simple approach to improve catalytic activity of MOF-derived nanoparticles. More importantly, the delicate assembly based on MOFs would not only provide more possibility on controllable self-assembly but also demonstrate the uniqueness and irreplaceability of MOFs in chemical and material science.
